1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
He tamed lightning and built the world's first battery.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,360
He created an instrument that inspired Mozart, currency that

3
00:00:14,361 --> 00:00:18,160
foiled counterfeiters, and a stove that fought off the coldest

4
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:19,760
winter.

5
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:24,280
This founding father was one of his era's greatest scientists

6
00:00:24,281 --> 00:00:27,560
and an inventor whose vision still shines today.

7
00:00:27,561 --> 00:00:31,060
Now, Ben Franklin Tech on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:53,320
Find the highest point on any tall structure,

9
00:00:53,321 --> 00:00:55,620
and you'll see an ordinary-looking device

10
00:00:55,621 --> 00:01:00,420
hidden among the antennas and decorative spires.

11
00:01:00,421 --> 00:01:04,280
The unremarkable metal point, known as the lightning rod,

12
00:01:04,281 --> 00:01:07,780
was once the ultimate symbol of science's triumph

13
00:01:07,781 --> 00:01:10,160
and a reminder of the terrifying power of nature.

14
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:19,080
Invented in 1749, it was the first practical application

15
00:01:19,081 --> 00:01:22,520
of the once mysterious force known as electricity.

16
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:31,320
Today, lightning rods protect millions of structures, large and small, in the United States alone.

17
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:42,460
You'll find one at the top of one Liberty Place, the 950-foot skyscraper that looks down over Philadelphia,

18
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:48,040
where the lightning rod was invented by the city's most famous resident, Benjamin Franklin.

19
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:54,800
His scientific genius made him a mythic figure.

20
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:58,240
while his practical inventions and straight talk

21
00:01:58,241 --> 00:02:01,900
made him an intimate friend of the people to this day.

22
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:09,520
Although many people are familiar with this image of Franklin,

23
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,020
they may not be aware of what Franklin actually did

24
00:02:16,021 --> 00:02:18,500
to bring electricity down from the heavens

25
00:02:18,501 --> 00:02:21,120
and eventually into our daily lives.

26
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:36,080
17-year-old Franklin arrived in the bustling city of Philadelphia in 1723.

27
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:43,460
He was a destitute runaway from his older brother's printing shop in Boston.

28
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:49,780
Within a decade, through his potent combination of skill, hard work, and business sense,

29
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:52,920
Franklin emerged as the leading printer in Philadelphia.

30
00:02:52,921 --> 00:02:59,760
At the age of 42, he retired, a wealthy man in the city of brotherly love.

31
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:05,460
With his printing empire established throughout the colonies,

32
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:10,940
Franklin was now free, he said, to devote his life to science and the improvement of mankind.

33
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:18,160
It was the peak of the Enlightenment, the age of Voltaire, Hume, and Rousseau,

34
00:03:18,161 --> 00:03:21,920
when philosophers championed reason and empirical data

35
00:03:21,921 --> 00:03:24,040
over scripture and superstition.

36
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:27,000
But it was sort of rough and tumble.

37
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,260
And so you could have people who, anyone who was like a gentleman,

38
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:31,860
which meant you didn't have to work for a living,

39
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:35,840
was sort of expected almost to be dabbling in science.

40
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:37,900
And I think Franklin kind of grew up in that era

41
00:03:37,901 --> 00:03:40,200
and wouldn't have hesitated for a moment

42
00:03:40,201 --> 00:03:42,400
to feel that he himself could also be involved.

43
00:03:42,401 --> 00:03:46,400
Franklin threw himself into the most exciting area

44
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,340
of 18th century science, the study of electrical fire.

45
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:56,840
He had first been drawn to electricity in 1743,

46
00:03:56,940 --> 00:04:00,160
when he witnessed an electrical oddity show put on by a visiting

47
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:02,060
Scottish performer.

48
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,820
Before Franklin came along, electricity was sort of parlor

49
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,760
tricks, people doing static electricity and making sparks.

50
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:09,760
But Franklin tried to make it useful.

51
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:20,260
Like a 21st century techie, Franklin bought or built all the latest electrical gadgetry, including this electrostatic generator.

52
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:30,800
The way the various electrostatic generators work is that if you rotate this drum, it will pick up electrons, electrical charge, from the pad.

53
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:41,160
The charge is transferred through the discharge tower, and it comes to the front of the discharge tower at this point right here where you'll see a little bolt.

54
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:51,860
Franklin's device was arranged in a more vertical fashion and used a glass sphere with brass fingers and a leather pad to generate the static electricity.

55
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:58,720
Franklin could then collect the electricity in a glass and metal container called a Leiden jar for use in experiments.

56
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:04,840
Franklin began his electrical experiments in earnest in 1745.

57
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:12,040
In just seven years, he would turn electricity from a parlor trick to a revolutionary science.

58
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:20,300
Franklin's most significant theoretical breakthrough was to determine that everything in the world contained electricity.

59
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:25,660
You don't create electricity out of thin air.

60
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:28,600
It actually is coming from something else, going to something else.

61
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:33,580
And the whole system remains at a constant amount of electricity.

62
00:05:33,581 --> 00:05:35,660
It's just moving from one point to another.

63
00:05:35,661 --> 00:05:41,760
And this was a great breakthrough at the time because people had no idea where electrical charge came from.

64
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:45,800
Known today as the conservation of electric charge,

65
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:48,780
it became the backbone of modern electrical science.

66
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:52,540
Franklin also challenged the prevailing theory

67
00:05:52,541 --> 00:05:55,020
that there were two different types of electricity.

68
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:58,420
The theory was that they would come together

69
00:05:58,421 --> 00:06:00,840
and one would cancel out the other.

70
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:02,980
So Franklin comes along and he says,

71
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:04,280
well, I don't think it's like that.

72
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:05,560
I think there's just one fluid,

73
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:08,920
but it can be positively or negatively charged

74
00:06:08,921 --> 00:06:11,640
and it can flow off an object onto another object.

75
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:14,520
While tinkering with his Leiden jars,

76
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:19,020
Franklin invented a device that would eventually become one of the most practical ever.

77
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:24,040
Now, as you can see, one jar doesn't really provide very much charge.

78
00:06:24,041 --> 00:06:29,540
So one of the things that Franklin figured out to do is to connect a number of these jars together

79
00:06:29,541 --> 00:06:33,640
so that you would have a large amount of charge in one spot.

80
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:38,680
In doing so, he could generate a much larger electrical shock discharge.

81
00:06:38,681 --> 00:06:41,080
Franklin called his device a battery,

82
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:43,560
since it acted like a battery of cannon.

83
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:45,480
Indeed, on at least one occasion,

84
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:49,860
this battery knocked its inventor unconscious.

85
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:52,520
The world's first electrician was most intrigued

86
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:55,900
with what he called the wonderful power of points.

87
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,600
A point like this sharp metal rod

88
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:03,640
has the ability to draw electricity off

89
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:07,640
of a highly charged conductor.

90
00:07:07,641 --> 00:07:10,640
Franklin believed that armed with the power of points,

91
00:07:10,641 --> 00:07:12,640
he could be the first to prove that the electric sparks

92
00:07:12,641 --> 00:07:16,640
in his laboratory and the lightning that came from clouds

93
00:07:16,641 --> 00:07:19,640
were one and the same.

94
00:07:19,641 --> 00:07:22,640
He has a wonderful line in his notebook.

95
00:07:22,641 --> 00:07:24,640
He says, let the experiments be made.

96
00:07:24,641 --> 00:07:27,640
So he comes up with an experiment, the Franklin experiment,

97
00:07:27,641 --> 00:07:30,640
to have a lightning rod to see if it will draw the charge

98
00:07:30,641 --> 00:07:33,640
from the bottom of a cloud.

99
00:07:33,641 --> 00:07:36,640
In his book on electricity, Franklin described what became

100
00:07:36,641 --> 00:07:40,880
described what became known as the sentry box experiment.

101
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:44,940
A Frenchman, Thomas Francois Dallabard, who had translated

102
00:07:44,941 --> 00:07:48,580
Franklin's book, decided to try it.

103
00:07:48,581 --> 00:07:51,780
A sharp metallic rod extended from a wooden shed that

104
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:54,500
protected the observer from rain.

105
00:07:54,501 --> 00:07:58,100
If the rod, which was insulated from ground, drew off electricity

106
00:07:58,101 --> 00:08:01,940
from a storm cloud, the observer could draw off sparks with an

107
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:05,000
insulated ground wire.

108
00:08:05,001 --> 00:08:06,260
And the sparks flew.

109
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:15,720
Dalibard did the experiment successfully for the first time on May 10, 1752.

110
00:08:16,660 --> 00:08:24,300
Dalibard was careful to acknowledge that this experiment had, in fact, been suggested by Benjamin Franklin.

111
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:28,720
The sentry box made Franklin instantly famous throughout Europe.

112
00:08:28,721 --> 00:08:36,600
At about the same time, he performed his own, potentially very dangerous, test back in Philadelphia.

113
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:45,300
In place of a tall lightning rod, he thought that a small metal point on top of a kite

114
00:08:45,301 --> 00:08:51,180
would draw electrical fire from the storm clouds and conduct it to a key dangling from the end of the string.

115
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,260
When the key at the end of Franklin's kite string drew off sparks,

116
00:08:56,261 --> 00:08:59,400
It confirmed the results of the sentrybox experiment.

117
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:02,460
Thunderclouds contain electricity,

118
00:09:03,180 --> 00:09:05,400
and lightning is an electric discharge.

119
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:13,200
Thunder clouds become electrified by processes

120
00:09:13,201 --> 00:09:16,060
which we're still trying to understand better.

121
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:21,680
But what we do know is any time thunderclouds contain both ice

122
00:09:21,681 --> 00:09:28,940
and hail particles and supercooled liquid water drops,

123
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:33,040
those clouds generate a lot of electricity

124
00:09:33,041 --> 00:09:37,280
by collisions between the hail particles, the ice crystals,

125
00:09:37,281 --> 00:09:40,140
and in the presence of the supercooled water.

126
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:45,800
So you've got a negative charge accumulating at the bottom of the cloud,

127
00:09:45,860 --> 00:09:48,560
a positive charge accumulating at the top,

128
00:09:48,561 --> 00:09:54,180
and that charge difference creates a gigantic amount of voltage inside the cloud.

129
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:56,780
And that's the beginning of a stroke of lightning.

130
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:01,160
The lightning can discharge between the negative and positive portions of the cloud

131
00:10:01,161 --> 00:10:05,320
or between the cloud and the earth, which usually has a positive charge.

132
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:11,980
Proving the electrical properties of clouds was a breathtaking discovery.

133
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:17,080
But practical-minded Benjamin Franklin wasn't satisfied.

134
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:22,440
He said, we've studied the theory, but we don't have any practical use for it yet.

135
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:25,760
The practical use became the lightning rod.

136
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:30,460
Having proven that lightning was an electric discharge,

137
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:33,860
Franklin was confident that, like the sparks in his lab,

138
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:38,360
it could also be safely drawn off by a metal conductor that was connected to ground.

139
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:44,660
In 1752, he was able to convince other prominent Philadelphians

140
00:10:44,661 --> 00:10:47,420
to install lightning rods on important civic buildings.

141
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:50,860
The lightning rod stood on the rooftop.

142
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:52,480
It was bolted down.

143
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:57,320
It was stapled to a wire that went down the side of the house into the ground.

144
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:00,520
A very simple thing that any local blacksmith could make.

145
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:02,520
And yet what it did was something magnificent,

146
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:07,400
which was that it intercepted a thunderbolt that would be about to strike a building

147
00:11:07,401 --> 00:11:10,420
and channel it harmlessly into the ground.

148
00:11:10,421 --> 00:11:16,100
18th century buildings contained very little metal,

149
00:11:16,620 --> 00:11:19,160
no wiring, indoor plumbing, or steel supports.

150
00:11:21,140 --> 00:11:24,440
Lacking such conductors, the wood and brick structures

151
00:11:24,441 --> 00:11:26,000
took the brunt of a lightning strike

152
00:11:26,001 --> 00:11:28,740
and sometimes exploded into flames,

153
00:11:28,741 --> 00:11:30,400
which could spread to the next building.

154
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:36,980
Churches, usually the tallest structures around,

155
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:38,080
were especially vulnerable.

156
00:11:38,081 --> 00:11:42,000
Bell ringers were more vulnerable still.

157
00:11:44,620 --> 00:11:47,600
Superstition held that ringing the church bells during a storm

158
00:11:47,601 --> 00:11:49,060
would ward off the lightning.

159
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:52,980
Well, of course, what happened is a number of bell ringers were electrocuted.

160
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:57,240
So by the mid-18th century, you have governments in Europe issuing edicts

161
00:11:57,241 --> 00:12:00,000
saying, stop sending the bell ringers up into the tower

162
00:12:00,001 --> 00:12:01,900
because there were too many fatalities.

163
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,560
Yet many clerics objected to the lightning rod,

164
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:08,480
believing it was presumptuous, if not heretical,

165
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,540
to tamper with the ultimate expression of God's wrath.

166
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:15,320
But what Franklin basically said to those people is,

167
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:18,420
God doesn't mind if we put ruse over our head to keep out the rain.

168
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:23,240
I don't think God is going to mind if we erect a lightning rod to protect our families.

169
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:25,020
And it worked.

170
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:29,040
Lightning rods steadily proliferated across America and Europe.

171
00:12:31,220 --> 00:12:34,660
When a few early brass rods melted from lightning strikes,

172
00:12:34,661 --> 00:12:38,620
Franklin made an improved design with a more substantial conductor

173
00:12:38,621 --> 00:12:42,060
and a larger rod that was buried deeper in the ground.

174
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:49,820
That forms, in fact, the basis for virtually every lightning protection code in the world today.

175
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:55,180
Franklin also built the world's first lightning detector,

176
00:12:55,740 --> 00:12:59,520
which would sound an alarm when it drew a charge from his lightning rod during a storm.

177
00:12:59,521 --> 00:13:10,060
As each bell is charged and then discharged, it attracts the clapper in the middle so that it rings one bell on one side, then rings the other bell on the other side.

178
00:13:10,460 --> 00:13:16,200
And thus that electrical charge could ring the bells and demonstrate to Franklin when lightning was approaching.

179
00:13:17,980 --> 00:13:21,665
Taming lightning had repercussions beyond science and safety.

180
00:13:21,666 --> 00:13:23,840
It was a major metaphysical triumph.

181
00:13:23,841 --> 00:13:30,200
One of the great things about the Enlightenment is that it said we could have some control over our environment.

182
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:36,620
That lightning wasn't just God's anger and storms weren't the wrath of the devils or the gods or whatever.

183
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:39,300
And a political triumph.

184
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:42,780
Franklin's breakthrough made him a folk hero in France,

185
00:13:43,080 --> 00:13:47,040
where it paved the way for his critical work securing support for the American Revolution.

186
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:53,280
But the relationship between Franklin's science and his politics was deeper still.

187
00:13:53,840 --> 00:13:59,300
The French said of him, he snatched lightning from the sky and the scepter from tyrants.

188
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:04,200
Science, and particularly someone like Franklin, who was a symbol for this,

189
00:14:04,660 --> 00:14:08,120
became a kind of a template for political and social ambitions.

190
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:13,380
In other words, if the empiricism and reason of science can deliver us these kind of things,

191
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:17,960
like the lightning rod, what would happen if we applied it to the way society was organized?

192
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:23,800
Franklin's wildly inventive mind wasn't content with one area of study.

193
00:14:23,840 --> 00:14:30,240
and electrical fire wasn't the only type of fire to fascinate him

194
00:14:30,241 --> 00:14:37,540
he would spend half his life improving ways to heat people's homes

195
00:14:37,541 --> 00:14:42,960
franklin refused to patent his lightning rod or his other inventions

196
00:14:42,961 --> 00:14:47,200
saying as we enjoy great advantages from the inventions of others

197
00:14:47,201 --> 00:14:51,820
we should be glad of an opportunity to serve others by an invention of ours

198
00:14:51,821 --> 00:14:55,820
Ben Franklin Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

199
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:06,980
In his long life, Benjamin Franklin became the most

200
00:15:06,981 --> 00:15:10,080
successful printer, author, and publisher in the New World,

201
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:15,020
a key contributor to America's founding documents,

202
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:19,080
one of history's most successful diplomats,

203
00:15:19,081 --> 00:15:23,240
and one of the 18th century's greatest scientists.

204
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:29,360
And yet his most enduring interest, spanning six decades,

205
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:33,820
was how to build a technologically superior fireplace.

206
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:37,860
The obsession began in 1739.

207
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:43,380
When not operating his printing empire or serving as postmaster for Philadelphia,

208
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:47,420
Franklin worked on a design to improve the open hearth fireplace,

209
00:15:47,421 --> 00:15:49,080
favored by English colonists.

210
00:15:52,860 --> 00:15:54,580
Despite its cheery appearance,

211
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:57,500
the open fireplace was a miserable heating solution

212
00:15:57,501 --> 00:15:59,420
for the northern colony's long winters.

213
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:02,740
Traditionally, the open hearth in a fireplace,

214
00:16:03,420 --> 00:16:06,000
most of the heat passes upward with the smoke

215
00:16:06,001 --> 00:16:07,260
and directly out the chimney.

216
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:12,260
The fireplace design doesn't reflect heat out into the room,

217
00:16:12,261 --> 00:16:14,820
and it certainly doesn't radiate heat out into the room.

218
00:16:14,821 --> 00:16:20,180
The challenge of heating a house more efficiently fit perfectly into Franklin's interests.

219
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:27,920
It combined a hot 18th century scientific topic, the thermodynamic properties of air,

220
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:33,860
with a practical objective, making people's lives healthier and more comfortable.

221
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:41,460
It even tapped into Franklin's passion for what we would now consider environmentalism.

222
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:50,020
He called the fruit of his labor the Pennsylvania fireplace.

223
00:16:50,021 --> 00:16:53,060
Well, here at the Mercer Museum,

224
00:16:53,061 --> 00:16:56,860
we have what's been called the only original Franklin stove

225
00:16:56,861 --> 00:16:58,940
for Pennsylvania fireplace, as he called it,

226
00:16:58,941 --> 00:17:01,140
based on his original design.

227
00:17:01,141 --> 00:17:03,500
Franklin's design for the Pennsylvania fireplace

228
00:17:03,501 --> 00:17:07,580
sought to combine the efficiency of the closed stove

229
00:17:07,581 --> 00:17:10,820
that was typical of Europe and the Pennsylvania German settlers

230
00:17:10,821 --> 00:17:12,980
in the early part of the 18th century

231
00:17:12,981 --> 00:17:15,640
with the open hearth that was favored by the English.

232
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:19,860
Franklin had noted that the German-style closed stoves

233
00:17:19,861 --> 00:17:21,440
conducted more heat into the room

234
00:17:21,441 --> 00:17:23,200
since they stood out from the wall

235
00:17:23,201 --> 00:17:25,980
and were made of iron, a good conductor.

236
00:17:28,860 --> 00:17:31,660
But Franklin's hybrid fireplace does much more

237
00:17:31,661 --> 00:17:34,100
than combine an iron stove with an open hearth.

238
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,280
Its first major innovation is called the airbox,

239
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:42,360
a baffled chamber sitting just behind the fire.

240
00:17:42,980 --> 00:17:49,260
An opening at the bottom of the airbox brings in cool, but not cold, air from the basement or room.

241
00:17:50,140 --> 00:17:57,400
This smoke-free air is heated by the fire, rises through holes at the top of the stove, and is pushed into the room.

242
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:06,400
Franklin's fireplace is also designed to squeeze as much heating potential as possible from wood before it escapes in the form of smoke.

243
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:12,400
Franklin was very economically minded. A penny saved is a penny earned.

244
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:18,160
He tried to use the wood efficiently and burn it efficiently.

245
00:18:18,161 --> 00:18:21,960
In addition, there would be less smoke produced

246
00:18:21,961 --> 00:18:25,960
because smoke was just unburned wood.

247
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:29,580
And Franklin recognized that.

248
00:18:30,180 --> 00:18:33,680
The Franklin stove entices the smoke to linger a bit longer

249
00:18:33,681 --> 00:18:36,800
through its second key innovation, the air siphon.

250
00:18:37,940 --> 00:18:41,840
Inside the air siphon, smoke from the fire rises up and over the airbox.

251
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:46,080
It's then pulled down through the bottom of the stove by the

252
00:18:46,081 --> 00:18:50,120
greater upward siphoning action of the chimney, all the while

253
00:18:50,121 --> 00:18:54,020
conducting heat into the room through the stove's walls.

254
00:18:54,021 --> 00:18:57,020
By keeping the hot air in the room longer, the Franklin stove

255
00:18:57,021 --> 00:19:02,040
used up to 75% less wood than an open fireplace, a marvel of

256
00:19:02,041 --> 00:19:03,040
efficiency.

257
00:19:03,041 --> 00:19:07,040
It also emitted much less smoke from the chimney.

258
00:19:07,041 --> 00:19:10,080
In fact, Franklin wrote with great foresight about not only

259
00:19:10,081 --> 00:19:12,720
foresight about not only the need to make our air less

260
00:19:12,721 --> 00:19:15,920
polluted, but also the importance of conserving the

261
00:19:15,921 --> 00:19:22,800
nation's fuel and its forests, all critical concerns today.

262
00:19:22,801 --> 00:19:25,520
Franklin's own Pennsylvania fireplace worked to his great

263
00:19:25,521 --> 00:19:29,760
satisfaction, but when put into the marketplace in 1741, it

264
00:19:29,761 --> 00:19:32,960
flopped. What probably happened, those

265
00:19:32,961 --> 00:19:36,640
people, those few people who actually acquired an original

266
00:19:36,641 --> 00:19:39,600
Franklin, Pennsylvania fireplace design, is that the

267
00:19:39,601 --> 00:19:44,800
the smoke backed up into the room and so his initial design failed he didn't sell many of the

268
00:19:44,801 --> 00:19:50,480
many of the stoves franklin later found ways to improve the air siphon and keep smoke out of the

269
00:19:50,481 --> 00:19:56,160
room but the design was still too tricky for a successful consumer product

270
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:03,040
he continued to design stoves including a portable stove with a rotating grate

271
00:20:03,041 --> 00:20:06,240
and a smokeless coal-burning stove.

272
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:11,280
Well, in terms of the legacy of the Franklin stove,

273
00:20:11,520 --> 00:20:14,960
most of what we know of today or think of today as Franklin

274
00:20:14,961 --> 00:20:19,100
stoves are simply iron hearths that are inserted

275
00:20:19,101 --> 00:20:20,780
into a traditional fireplace.

276
00:20:21,540 --> 00:20:24,600
While the modern Franklin stove is a misnomer,

277
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:28,100
the bold design of the original did influence the evolution

278
00:20:28,101 --> 00:20:29,100
of heating technology.

279
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:38,140
Franklin's interest in fire and his concern over saving fuel carried over

280
00:20:38,141 --> 00:20:43,860
into his design for a new and improved street lamp. Previously the lamps had

281
00:20:43,861 --> 00:20:49,160
been globes and there were two problems with these globes they were sealed and

282
00:20:49,161 --> 00:20:54,460
they became very smoky and didn't put out much light and then furthermore if a

283
00:20:54,461 --> 00:20:58,400
vandal were to break one of those globes you'd have to replace the whole piece of

284
00:20:58,401 --> 00:21:02,000
Well, he came up with this idea of a four-sided lamp.

285
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:04,040
First of all, one piece gets broken.

286
00:21:04,140 --> 00:21:06,340
You only have to fix one out of four.

287
00:21:06,440 --> 00:21:08,680
And furthermore, because there was a chimney up

288
00:21:08,780 --> 00:21:12,620
in the center of it, it meant that you had better light.

289
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:14,520
And that improved safety.

290
00:21:14,620 --> 00:21:19,080
I mean, there are so many layers to his improvements.

291
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:21,560
And also, it leads into his interest

292
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:24,420
in sort of environmentalism overall.

293
00:21:24,520 --> 00:21:27,120
Franklin's most enduring legacy as an environmentalist

294
00:21:27,121 --> 00:21:32,960
environmentalist may be a concept that occurred to him in Paris in 1783 while negotiating the

295
00:21:32,961 --> 00:21:35,560
final peace treaty with England.

296
00:21:35,561 --> 00:21:40,840
Industrious even at age 78, the former printer was taken aback by

297
00:21:40,841 --> 00:21:46,360
the late-to-bed, late-to-rise habits of the French aristocracy, who burned expensive candles into the

298
00:21:46,361 --> 00:21:50,325
wee hours and wasted the free light of the morning sun.

299
00:21:50,326 --> 00:21:53,280
So he had the idea, and it was actually a

300
00:21:53,281 --> 00:21:56,520
to kind of shift the working hours in the summer where you

301
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:59,680
wouldn't need to burn so many candles at night.

302
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:02,080
But Franklin's idea of changing the clock

303
00:22:02,180 --> 00:22:06,920
to save fuel wouldn't take hold until World War I, as Europe

304
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:10,740
grappled with the high cost of fuel.

305
00:22:10,840 --> 00:22:12,640
In America, an extra hour of daylight

306
00:22:12,740 --> 00:22:15,440
was enacted year round for the duration of the Second World

307
00:22:15,540 --> 00:22:16,240
War.

308
00:22:18,540 --> 00:22:23,280
Congress called the policy daylight saving time.

309
00:22:23,281 --> 00:22:28,020
In the post-war years, daylight saving time gradually shifted

310
00:22:28,021 --> 00:22:30,020
into its current spring to autumn slot.

311
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:35,640
It may be impossible to calculate the amount of fuel it saved over the decades,

312
00:22:35,980 --> 00:22:40,940
but a study during the 1970s oil crunch found that one extra hour of daylight

313
00:22:40,941 --> 00:22:44,760
saved the equivalent of 10,000 barrels of oil per day.

314
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:47,400
Surely Ben Franklin would have approved.

315
00:22:47,401 --> 00:22:51,040
As fascinated as Franklin was with fire,

316
00:22:51,140 --> 00:22:55,340
he was equally entranced by the properties of water.

317
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:02,940
And we still see and hear the results today.

318
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:12,960
In 1786, the year before he participated

319
00:23:13,060 --> 00:23:16,200
in drafting the Constitution, 80-year-old Ben

320
00:23:16,201 --> 00:23:18,540
80-year-old Ben Franklin published a paper

321
00:23:18,640 --> 00:23:21,540
on the nine causes of smoky fireplaces

322
00:23:21,640 --> 00:23:23,900
and a cure for each of them.

323
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,740
Ben Franklin Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

324
00:23:30,140 --> 00:23:32,740
At the National Hurricane Center in Miami,

325
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:35,560
specialists work at the cutting edge of weather prediction

326
00:23:35,660 --> 00:23:36,760
24 hours a day.

327
00:23:40,600 --> 00:23:45,500
High-tech tools, from satellite imagery and Doppler radar

328
00:23:45,501 --> 00:23:47,400
to a bird's-eye view of the storm,

329
00:23:48,160 --> 00:23:51,400
give these meteorologists a wide range of data to work with

330
00:23:51,401 --> 00:23:54,280
as they accurately forecast nature's fury.

331
00:23:56,720 --> 00:23:59,120
But the first modern weatherman

332
00:23:59,121 --> 00:24:01,900
relied solely on acute powers of observation.

333
00:24:02,580 --> 00:24:04,300
His name was Benjamin Franklin.

334
00:24:04,900 --> 00:24:07,320
First of all, Franklin is really the father of meteorology

335
00:24:07,321 --> 00:24:09,180
because he explained where lightning came from.

336
00:24:09,620 --> 00:24:12,240
So that alone is enough to guarantee him his place

337
00:24:12,241 --> 00:24:13,700
in the firmament of weathermen.

338
00:24:13,701 --> 00:24:17,660
However, Franklin did something really ingenious

339
00:24:17,661 --> 00:24:21,840
when it comes to understanding how storms travel across North America.

340
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:29,680
One night in 1743, Franklin was preparing to watch a lunar eclipse,

341
00:24:30,500 --> 00:24:32,340
but his view was obscured by a storm.

342
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:37,680
Up until Franklin's time, it was assumed that storms blew in from the northeast.

343
00:24:37,980 --> 00:24:39,540
Even the name nor'easter is used.

344
00:24:39,541 --> 00:24:42,620
because that's the way the wind comes down

345
00:24:42,621 --> 00:24:44,180
towards New England and into Pennsylvania

346
00:24:44,181 --> 00:24:45,360
when there's a storm.

347
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:47,760
He was surprised to read in the newspapers

348
00:24:47,761 --> 00:24:51,060
that the people in Boston saw this eclipse perfectly well.

349
00:24:51,740 --> 00:24:55,720
So what he did was he wrote to all of his correspondents

350
00:24:55,721 --> 00:24:57,780
up and down the Atlantic seaboard.

351
00:24:58,300 --> 00:24:59,680
By charting their responses,

352
00:25:00,360 --> 00:25:01,580
Franklin was able to determine

353
00:25:01,581 --> 00:25:03,160
that the storm had actually moved

354
00:25:03,161 --> 00:25:04,820
from southwest to northeast.

355
00:25:05,560 --> 00:25:07,260
He then theorized, correctly,

356
00:25:07,261 --> 00:25:11,100
that the movement of weather is controlled by differences in air pressure.

357
00:25:12,600 --> 00:25:17,140
Most people today view that particular discovery

358
00:25:17,141 --> 00:25:20,100
as the foundation of modern meteorology.

359
00:25:21,560 --> 00:25:25,020
Franklin would publish some of the first weather and climate forecasts

360
00:25:25,021 --> 00:25:26,700
in his Poor Richard's Almanac.

361
00:25:28,040 --> 00:25:31,940
He also wrote with surprising accuracy about global climate change.

362
00:25:33,100 --> 00:25:36,100
During the unusually cold and foggy summer of 1783,

363
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:41,060
He may have been the first to connect climate change with atmospheric pollution

364
00:25:41,061 --> 00:25:45,000
when he correctly linked the cool weather to a volcanic eruption

365
00:25:45,001 --> 00:25:47,480
that had taken place in Iceland several months earlier.

366
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:57,340
Franklin even speculated about the effect of deforestation on the planet's climate.

367
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:06,520
In the 21st century, as global warming threatens to redraw our climate zones,

368
00:26:07,260 --> 00:26:10,560
Climatologists are closely watching the Gulf Stream,

369
00:26:10,561 --> 00:26:13,300
the warm Atlantic ocean current that brings temperate weather

370
00:26:13,301 --> 00:26:16,260
to northern Europe.

371
00:26:16,261 --> 00:26:18,800
Not surprisingly, it was the father of meteorology,

372
00:26:18,801 --> 00:26:23,100
Ben Franklin, who first mapped the Gulf Stream.

373
00:26:23,101 --> 00:26:26,220
Franklin first heard of this warm current in the 1760s

374
00:26:26,221 --> 00:26:32,300
from whaling captains, including his cousin, Timothy Folger.

375
00:26:32,301 --> 00:26:34,220
On his many transatlantic journeys,

376
00:26:34,221 --> 00:26:37,620
Franklin painstakingly measured ocean temperatures.

377
00:26:37,621 --> 00:26:39,620
When he'd go across the ocean, he'd take a barrel

378
00:26:39,621 --> 00:26:41,420
and he'd lower it into the ocean.

379
00:26:41,421 --> 00:26:44,220
It had a valve so he could measure the temperature of the water

380
00:26:44,221 --> 00:26:47,620
at each depth, because he wanted to see how deep the Gulf Stream went,

381
00:26:47,621 --> 00:26:49,220
not just where it went.

382
00:26:49,221 --> 00:26:53,620
In 1768, Franklin and Timothy Folger unveiled their chart,

383
00:26:53,621 --> 00:26:56,420
along with the name Gulf Stream.

384
00:26:56,421 --> 00:26:58,820
And that chart has held up amazingly well.

385
00:26:58,821 --> 00:27:01,820
Orbiting spacecraft in our own day and age,

386
00:27:01,821 --> 00:27:07,700
Their observations of the Gulf Stream correspond almost exactly with the chart Franklin left us.

387
00:27:10,520 --> 00:27:14,020
To this day, eastbound ships take advantage of the current,

388
00:27:14,660 --> 00:27:17,240
while scientists continue to study it.

389
00:27:18,460 --> 00:27:21,060
Franklin's interest in water began at age 11.

390
00:27:22,060 --> 00:27:26,420
An excellent swimmer, young Ben invented a pair of swim paddles for his hands

391
00:27:26,421 --> 00:27:28,660
so he could cut through the water more efficiently.

392
00:27:28,661 --> 00:27:32,660
Franklin designed a wide range of maritime innovations,

393
00:27:32,661 --> 00:27:34,660
including a primitive manually-pumped version

394
00:27:34,661 --> 00:27:38,660
of a jet boat, a canvas sea anchor to keep boats

395
00:27:38,661 --> 00:27:43,660
from drifting in stormy waters, and even spill-proof soup bowls

396
00:27:43,661 --> 00:27:45,660
for rough ocean crossings.

397
00:27:47,660 --> 00:27:49,660
Franklin's love of water indirectly inspired

398
00:27:49,661 --> 00:27:52,660
what he considered his favorite invention.

399
00:27:58,660 --> 00:28:06,120
While living in England in 1757, Franklin attended a concert in which a musician performed melodies on the rims of wine glasses.

400
00:28:08,540 --> 00:28:10,940
Franklin loved the sound of the vibrating glass.

401
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:16,200
But being Franklin, wondered if there might be a better way to produce it.

402
00:28:16,720 --> 00:28:20,180
So he thought, first of all, the water tuning's got to go way too much trouble.

403
00:28:20,540 --> 00:28:24,600
So if you make the glasses the correct size in the first place, then you can dispense with that,

404
00:28:24,601 --> 00:28:28,820
and you'll end up with a set ranging from a relatively large glass for your base end

405
00:28:28,821 --> 00:28:30,500
to small glasses for the high end.

406
00:28:31,100 --> 00:28:35,540
This would allow a musician to play complex chords in addition to single-note melodies.

407
00:28:36,440 --> 00:28:38,420
Instead of the musician rotating his fingers,

408
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:42,520
37 glasses of varying diameter and thickness would rotate,

409
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:44,680
turned by a foot-powered spindle.

410
00:28:46,060 --> 00:28:50,440
In 1761, Franklin unveiled his glass harmonica.

411
00:28:54,600 --> 00:29:05,060
Its ethereal sound created nothing less than a sensation in Europe.

412
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:11,040
Marie Antoinette played the instrument.

413
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:17,980
The famous German physician, Franz Mesmer, used the harmonica's heavenly tones to mesmerize his patients.

414
00:29:20,920 --> 00:29:24,320
The instrument also mesmerized a young composer.

415
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:29,140
Mozart's first exposure was actually at Mesmer's house.

416
00:29:29,141 --> 00:29:32,140
The Mesmers and the Mozarts were friends.

417
00:29:32,580 --> 00:29:36,500
Mozart wrote two pieces for the glass harmonica, including this one.

418
00:29:42,980 --> 00:29:46,240
Beethoven and Haydn also composed for Franklin's instrument.

419
00:29:51,720 --> 00:29:54,180
Franklin himself excelled at it.

420
00:29:54,181 --> 00:29:56,380
He took one with him often when he traveled.

421
00:29:56,580 --> 00:29:58,580
He brought it back to Philadelphia.

422
00:29:59,220 --> 00:30:02,320
There's a story that his wife Deborah woke up one night

423
00:30:02,321 --> 00:30:04,260
and she heard the music of the harmonica.

424
00:30:04,360 --> 00:30:07,720
It was Ben practicing and she assumed she had died and gone to heaven.

425
00:30:13,440 --> 00:30:17,760
Despite the wild enthusiasm, the instrument's life was surprisingly brief.

426
00:30:18,600 --> 00:30:20,420
They eventually sort of lost out to the piano,

427
00:30:20,421 --> 00:30:24,120
which had come on strong by the late 18th, early 19th century.

428
00:30:24,180 --> 00:30:29,560
A much louder, much more adaptable instrument to the larger and larger orchestras that were being used.

429
00:30:29,940 --> 00:30:34,040
The harmonica was always very much kind of a very quiet chamber-like instrument.

430
00:30:35,220 --> 00:30:41,460
There were also rumors that the harmonica's tones, or perhaps the lead used in the glass, caused insanity.

431
00:30:42,440 --> 00:30:44,340
Personally, I think they have that backwards.

432
00:30:44,680 --> 00:30:47,500
I think you start a little crazy and then you want to play this.

433
00:30:47,501 --> 00:30:52,500
Armonica virtuoso William Zeitler has made it his mission to

434
00:30:52,501 --> 00:30:54,500
resurrect this unique instrument.

435
00:30:54,501 --> 00:30:58,500
He performs around the world and composes original pieces for

436
00:30:58,501 --> 00:30:59,500
it.

437
00:30:59,501 --> 00:31:01,500
I'd really like to get this instrument off of the

438
00:31:01,501 --> 00:31:03,500
endangered musical instrument list.

439
00:31:07,500 --> 00:31:10,500
There aren't many places to find precision-tuned glass bowls

440
00:31:10,501 --> 00:31:14,500
these days, so Zeitler turned to Cal glass in Costa Mesa,

441
00:31:14,501 --> 00:31:18,180
of California, which normally makes glass scientific instruments.

442
00:31:21,900 --> 00:31:27,540
After being shaped on a lathe, each glass bowl is sanded until it reaches the perfect pitch.

443
00:31:32,340 --> 00:31:35,420
With a new wave of harmonica players and manufacturers,

444
00:31:36,180 --> 00:31:40,200
this Enlightenment-era instrument may be ready to mesmerize the world again.

445
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:58,500
Franklin's inventions reached from music salons to the heavens.

446
00:32:00,300 --> 00:32:03,720
Yet through it all, he remained B. Franklin, printer.

447
00:32:05,220 --> 00:32:10,400
And as a printer, he not only made money, he made money harder to fake.

448
00:32:10,401 --> 00:32:18,320
In 1968, Benjamin Franklin was elected to the International Swimming Hall of Fame

449
00:32:18,321 --> 00:32:22,680
for devising paddles and flippers and promoting the health benefits of swimming.

450
00:32:23,400 --> 00:32:25,960
Ben Franklin Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

451
00:32:29,620 --> 00:32:34,000
It's entirely fitting that one of the world's most coveted pieces of paper,

452
00:32:34,400 --> 00:32:38,560
and one of the hardest to imitate, bears the image of one of history's greatest printers.

453
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:45,200
Printing allowed Franklin to become Franklin.

454
00:32:46,260 --> 00:32:53,680
So Franklin as a printer would eventually become the most successful in North America.

455
00:32:54,340 --> 00:32:58,780
Many would argue he was also the best in terms of the quality of the work that he produced.

456
00:32:59,860 --> 00:33:06,220
It was a very valuable occupation to have because, of course, it puts you right in the middle of the flow of information.

457
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:09,320
And Franklin himself, he took a printing press with him wherever he went.

458
00:33:09,321 --> 00:33:13,880
The press became Franklin's jumping-off point for the world of science and invention.

459
00:33:16,540 --> 00:33:20,320
And while the breakthroughs with electricity may be his greatest accomplishments,

460
00:33:21,180 --> 00:33:24,520
his work as a printer has also shaped our world.

461
00:33:26,020 --> 00:33:28,900
His real bread and butter was the Pennsylvania Gazette.

462
00:33:29,640 --> 00:33:31,420
That was his main source of income.

463
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:33,500
It became the leading newspaper in the colonies.

464
00:33:34,100 --> 00:33:36,800
People subscribed to it, not only in Philadelphia and Pennsylvania,

465
00:33:36,801 --> 00:33:38,700
but all over the 13 colonies.

466
00:33:39,320 --> 00:33:46,800
Franklin opened satellite print shops across the colonies, and as the colony's deputy postmaster,

467
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:49,800
he was able to link his print shops with the mail service.

468
00:33:51,900 --> 00:33:57,560
The combination of the printing and postmaster network was, for its time, the most advanced

469
00:33:57,561 --> 00:34:03,420
communications network anywhere, not just in the colonies, but I might even say even in Europe,

470
00:34:03,520 --> 00:34:05,900
there wasn't anything quite so far-flung and sophisticated.

471
00:34:05,901 --> 00:34:09,900
Franklin's innovations in the technology of printing came in

472
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,700
the area of currency.

473
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:16,340
His first, at age 22, was to convince the Pennsylvania

474
00:34:16,440 --> 00:34:19,440
colony that paper money was a good idea.

475
00:34:19,540 --> 00:34:23,520
Being a printer, he was eager to get the commission to print

476
00:34:23,620 --> 00:34:25,020
the currency.

477
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:27,960
And being the clever fellow he was,

478
00:34:28,060 --> 00:34:34,200
he published at his own expense a pamphlet expounding on his

479
00:34:34,201 --> 00:34:39,140
founding on his idea that there should be more paper currency than there presently was.

480
00:34:39,360 --> 00:34:41,820
And guess who got the commission to print it?

481
00:34:42,240 --> 00:34:43,000
He did.

482
00:34:45,940 --> 00:34:49,520
Franklin then began a lifelong battle against counterfeiters.

483
00:34:50,340 --> 00:34:53,320
In an era when each colony had its own currency,

484
00:34:54,060 --> 00:34:57,420
a large counterfeit operation could destabilize a colony's economy.

485
00:34:57,421 --> 00:35:04,420
Franklin's early anti-counterfeit strategies included ornamentation and hard-to-find fonts,

486
00:35:04,560 --> 00:35:07,306
and a not-so-subtle threat.

487
00:35:07,307 --> 00:35:14,120
He also had the idea of taking granulated mica flakes and embedding

488
00:35:14,121 --> 00:35:19,600
that and pressing that into the paper. He was always coming up with new ideas.

489
00:35:21,840 --> 00:35:25,900
Franklin's greatest contribution to counterfeit-proof currency was his introduction

490
00:35:25,901 --> 00:35:26,900
of leaf printing.

491
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:29,540
He got the idea from this book,

492
00:35:29,620 --> 00:35:31,820
which he saw in the library of James Logan.

493
00:35:32,160 --> 00:35:33,840
Franklin saw this book and was fascinated with it.

494
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:36,880
He and his friends went back to his printing office

495
00:35:36,881 --> 00:35:39,540
and began making nature prints of leaves

496
00:35:39,541 --> 00:35:40,740
on large sheets of paper,

497
00:35:40,940 --> 00:35:43,680
which he used to communicate botanical information

498
00:35:43,681 --> 00:35:46,940
to friends in London who were eager for any knowledge

499
00:35:46,941 --> 00:35:48,620
they could get about American plants.

500
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:51,560
Franklin realized that like a fingerprint,

501
00:35:52,100 --> 00:35:53,840
every plant's leaf pattern is unique

502
00:35:53,841 --> 00:35:55,980
and thus impossible to replicate.

503
00:35:56,980 --> 00:36:01,480
And so he got the idea of creating copper plates

504
00:36:01,481 --> 00:36:05,420
cast from actual leaves.

505
00:36:05,720 --> 00:36:09,640
And he used those leaf prints to make the paper

506
00:36:09,641 --> 00:36:11,460
that he printed his currency on.

507
00:36:11,960 --> 00:36:13,740
No one knows with certainty

508
00:36:13,741 --> 00:36:15,800
how Franklin made these engraving plates.

509
00:36:19,800 --> 00:36:21,920
He would have had to press the leaves

510
00:36:21,921 --> 00:36:23,520
into a soft plaster mold.

511
00:36:23,840 --> 00:36:27,260
then pour the molten metal to form the engraving plate.

512
00:36:27,940 --> 00:36:29,140
But there's a problem.

513
00:36:29,980 --> 00:36:31,600
Molten metal would crack the plaster.

514
00:36:33,020 --> 00:36:36,200
The theory is that Franklin added an insulating material,

515
00:36:36,820 --> 00:36:38,820
perhaps brick dust or asbestos,

516
00:36:39,240 --> 00:36:40,440
to keep the mold from breaking.

517
00:36:41,300 --> 00:36:46,060
Although he gave all his other inventions away freely,

518
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:48,120
this one he didn't,

519
00:36:48,900 --> 00:36:52,780
because obviously the counterfeiters would have used it.

520
00:36:53,840 --> 00:36:57,720
The very complicated engraved designs that we have in our money today,

521
00:36:57,721 --> 00:37:00,920
not unlike the patterns of veins on these leaves,

522
00:37:01,240 --> 00:37:04,500
are directly inspired by Franklin's leaf prints, I think.

523
00:37:05,220 --> 00:37:08,640
A lifetime of staring at currency patterns and fine print

524
00:37:08,641 --> 00:37:13,220
may have led the 78-year-old Franklin to one of his most enduring inventions.

525
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:21,840
They came out of his frustration when he was ambassador in France.

526
00:37:22,040 --> 00:37:23,080
His eyesight was failing.

527
00:37:23,920 --> 00:37:26,080
And almost every night he had a different social event,

528
00:37:26,140 --> 00:37:27,980
a dinner party to attend, that kind of thing.

529
00:37:28,180 --> 00:37:31,220
And what he found was that he couldn't both see the food on his plate

530
00:37:31,221 --> 00:37:35,220
and the faces of the people sitting across the table from him.

531
00:37:35,520 --> 00:37:37,760
And it was very important that he did see their faces

532
00:37:37,761 --> 00:37:39,680
because his French was not all that good.

533
00:37:39,980 --> 00:37:43,620
And he found it necessary to really be able to see people's lips when they spoke.

534
00:37:44,900 --> 00:37:47,600
Franklin realized that when using his reading glasses,

535
00:37:47,601 --> 00:37:49,880
he looked through the bottom half of his lenses.

536
00:37:50,700 --> 00:37:53,100
With his distance glasses, he looked through the top half.

537
00:37:53,720 --> 00:37:54,960
And then came the idea.

538
00:37:55,600 --> 00:37:59,420
Cut each lens in half and reattach them, one on top of the other.

539
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:03,220
So many of Franklin's inventions are things that, as soon as he invented them,

540
00:38:03,221 --> 00:38:05,980
you would think, oh, of course, it makes perfect sense.

541
00:38:06,200 --> 00:38:08,220
And bifocals are a really good example of that.

542
00:38:11,420 --> 00:38:14,540
Bifocals were the capping achievement for a visionary inventor.

543
00:38:15,320 --> 00:38:18,400
But Franklin's vision also encompassed entire systems.

544
00:38:19,880 --> 00:38:28,720
From fire departments and libraries to nations.

545
00:38:30,720 --> 00:38:41,380
Between 1728 and 1764, Franklin's hand-operated press printed about 2.5 million banknotes for Pennsylvania, Delaware, and New Jersey.

546
00:38:42,300 --> 00:38:44,960
Ben Franklin Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

547
00:38:44,961 --> 00:38:52,840
In today's America, pulsing with electronic communication and complex transportation systems,

548
00:38:53,680 --> 00:38:56,060
Benjamin Franklin would probably feel right at home.

549
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:59,920
If Franklin were alive today, he'd be a true technological geek.

550
00:39:00,200 --> 00:39:04,020
He'd be showing off his Blackberry or his Trio because he loved electricity,

551
00:39:04,200 --> 00:39:06,240
but he also loved the free flow of information.

552
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:07,520
He loved communicating.

553
00:39:07,760 --> 00:39:10,240
He loved the mail system, and he loved the email system.

554
00:39:10,400 --> 00:39:12,000
And certainly, he'd have a website.

555
00:39:14,960 --> 00:39:19,500
Franklin had a gift for inventing 18th century equivalents of such high-tech networks,

556
00:39:20,540 --> 00:39:23,180
complex systems that serve to improve society.

557
00:39:25,120 --> 00:39:27,880
He even helped invent a brand new type of nation.

558
00:39:30,580 --> 00:39:36,300
Franklin's first such idea was the Library Company of Philadelphia, which he founded at age 25.

559
00:39:38,060 --> 00:39:43,320
Anyone who bought a one-time share in the library could borrow a book and agree to return it later.

560
00:39:44,120 --> 00:39:45,260
You didn't return the book.

561
00:39:45,340 --> 00:39:46,440
You forfeited your deposit.

562
00:39:46,780 --> 00:39:48,220
It was in your interest to return the book.

563
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:49,900
When you returned it and you came back later,

564
00:39:49,940 --> 00:39:52,420
you could be sure it was going to be there again for you to look at.

565
00:39:52,980 --> 00:39:56,040
And this made it into a permanent institution.

566
00:39:56,800 --> 00:40:00,860
The library company was the first successful public lending library

567
00:40:00,861 --> 00:40:02,480
in the world that I know of.

568
00:40:02,660 --> 00:40:05,040
And I think that that was a great invention.

569
00:40:05,200 --> 00:40:07,720
I might even go so far as to say it's Franklin's greatest invention.

570
00:40:09,180 --> 00:40:12,220
This urge to democratize knowledge for the greater good

571
00:40:12,221 --> 00:40:14,980
grew out of Franklin's group of 12 fellow craftsmen,

572
00:40:16,260 --> 00:40:18,000
called the Leather Apron Club.

573
00:40:18,720 --> 00:40:21,000
The group was proudly named after the apron

574
00:40:21,001 --> 00:40:22,740
that identified them as skilled workers.

575
00:40:24,020 --> 00:40:26,800
The middling sort is what they would call themselves

576
00:40:26,801 --> 00:40:28,000
in the 18th century,

577
00:40:28,180 --> 00:40:33,700
but people with skills who weren't born wealthy,

578
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:34,900
who worked with their hands,

579
00:40:35,460 --> 00:40:37,540
the purpose of the Leather Apron Club,

580
00:40:37,600 --> 00:40:39,480
or the Junto, as it was later called,

581
00:40:39,481 --> 00:40:43,600
was to converse on Friday night over drink.

582
00:40:43,680 --> 00:40:45,180
They originally met at a nail house.

583
00:40:45,620 --> 00:40:48,360
And they would form these groups to apply technology

584
00:40:48,361 --> 00:40:50,240
into our daily lives,

585
00:40:50,280 --> 00:40:51,920
whether it was for a good fire department

586
00:40:51,921 --> 00:40:53,300
or a good lending library.

587
00:40:54,080 --> 00:40:55,640
Franklin was also a Freemason.

588
00:40:56,300 --> 00:40:57,560
And like Freemasonry,

589
00:40:57,561 --> 00:41:00,440
the Junto was originally a secret organization.

590
00:41:02,500 --> 00:41:06,280
It eventually became the American Philosophical Society,

591
00:41:06,760 --> 00:41:08,780
the nation's first learned society.

592
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:16,480
Franklin and his middling colleagues would also organize America's first volunteer militia,

593
00:41:16,481 --> 00:41:25,480
found the nation's first hospital, and create the first non-religious college in America.

594
00:41:25,481 --> 00:41:32,480
The inventor of the lightning rod took a comprehensive approach to fire prevention.

595
00:41:32,481 --> 00:41:41,520
He built his Philadelphia home using fire-resistant plaster to separate the brick from the wood.

596
00:41:44,220 --> 00:41:48,740
He and his fellow Junto members brought in state-of-the-art fire engines from England

597
00:41:48,741 --> 00:41:54,540
and organized bucket brigades into the city's first volunteer fire company.

598
00:41:54,541 --> 00:42:02,700
A tireless innovator in every stage of his career,

599
00:42:03,420 --> 00:42:06,120
Postmaster Franklin modernized the colony's mail system.

600
00:42:06,800 --> 00:42:15,080
He established a framework for the entire postal system

601
00:42:15,081 --> 00:42:19,400
that included the establishment of post offices,

602
00:42:19,401 --> 00:42:33,401
the mapping of routes that included determining distances between post offices and also setting rates for different increments along the postal routes.

603
00:42:33,820 --> 00:42:46,240
Do you know that during his time, he improved efficiency to the extent that it took one day to get a letter from Philadelphia to New York?

604
00:42:46,241 --> 00:42:50,920
we're lucky to get that kind of service today

605
00:42:50,921 --> 00:42:54,680
but the greatest system

606
00:42:54,681 --> 00:42:56,120
Benjamin Franklin invented

607
00:42:56,121 --> 00:42:58,480
was the United States itself

608
00:42:58,481 --> 00:43:00,800
of course he wasn't the only inventor

609
00:43:00,801 --> 00:43:05,240
but from his prescient attempts to unite the colonies

610
00:43:05,241 --> 00:43:06,660
in the 1750s

611
00:43:06,661 --> 00:43:11,500
through his brilliant treaty of amity with France

612
00:43:11,501 --> 00:43:15,200
to his critical great compromise

613
00:43:15,201 --> 00:43:18,140
that created two houses of Congress.

614
00:43:18,240 --> 00:43:21,300
Franklin was the great uniter.

615
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:25,340
One of Franklin's scientific principles was unification.

616
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:27,940
He loved to unify things, bring them together,

617
00:43:28,040 --> 00:43:30,680
so he'd had a single theory of electricity.

618
00:43:30,780 --> 00:43:32,580
But that also applied to his politics

619
00:43:32,680 --> 00:43:34,080
because he liked to bring colonies together.

620
00:43:34,180 --> 00:43:36,460
He liked to bring people together.

621
00:43:36,560 --> 00:43:38,820
The hand of Franklin the scientist

622
00:43:38,920 --> 00:43:41,000
may be most clearly seen in his notes

623
00:43:41,100 --> 00:43:45,200
on the first draft of the Declaration of Independence.

624
00:43:45,201 --> 00:43:48,040
Thomas Jefferson's original draft had read,

625
00:43:48,600 --> 00:43:51,340
We hold these truths to be sacred and undeniable.

626
00:43:52,340 --> 00:43:54,020
Franklin replaced the religious language

627
00:43:54,021 --> 00:43:56,840
with a phrase taken from scientific observation.

628
00:43:58,320 --> 00:44:01,040
Truths that are self-evident, that was Franklin's language.

629
00:44:01,120 --> 00:44:02,380
He took that directly from Newton.

630
00:44:02,920 --> 00:44:04,140
That's scientific language.

631
00:44:05,060 --> 00:44:07,420
It's in this sense that the lightning rod

632
00:44:07,421 --> 00:44:10,880
and the great American experiment in democracy are connected.

633
00:44:10,881 --> 00:44:14,880
just as Franklin the scientist.

